  "content": {
    "id": "p19-q9-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "9",
    "prompt": "A sample space $\\Omega$ has eight elements (with all elements equally likely) and a given event $A\\subseteq\\Omega$ has four elements. How many ways can you form a second event $B\\subseteq\\Omega$ such that $A$ and $B$ are independent?",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$70$ ways.\n\nChoose equal numbers of elements from $A$ and $A'$.",
      "worked": "Let $k=|A\\cap B|$ and $m=|B|$. Independence requires\n$$\\begin{align*}\\Pr(A\\cap B)&=\\Pr(A)\\Pr(B)\\\\\\frac{k}{8}&=\\frac48\\times\\frac{m}{8}\\\\m&=2k.\\end{align*}$$\nThus $B$ contains $k$ elements from $A$ and $k$ from $A'$, where $k=0,1,2,3,4$. The number of choices is\n$$\\begin{align*}N&=\\sum_{k=0}^{4}\\binom4k\\binom4k\\\\N&=1+16+36+16+1\\\\N&=70.\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 64
  },
